    ore than before (80 iron-vein tiles to 114), because the seam lands on ground nobody sweeps.

[DONE] Rocky patches of ground shouldn't be minable for stone — click-moving to a mountain
       defaults to harvesting it. Keep stone on the world's rocks only, so it stays finite and
       can be mined out locally.
    Rocky TERRAIN (tile type 2) counted as a stone source in three places: the worker's
    `findNode` search, the click resolver `gatherKindAt`, and an explicit exemption in the
    gather tick that stopped terrain-stone from ever depleting. All three now require an actual
    `rock` boulder. That is what made every mountainside a quarry — and a mountain is the
    biggest thing on the map, so a click meant to walk near one landed on "mine that instead".
    It also makes stone genuinely finite, which is what you asked for: a boulder is a node with
    a cap of 14 uses and a slow regrow, so a place can be worked out. There are ~43,000 boulders
    in the world and the nearest one to a town is 17-31 tiles, so stone is plentiful without
    being underfoot.

[DONE] Balance harness for the binding circle, and remains as upkeep.
    tools/host.js stands a raised host against a REAL town garrison — spawned through
    `spawnGuard`, so the arbalists and the merc mix are the game's — at four caster
    tiers, and reports the exchange rate in garrison dead per body of host lost. It
    also fights each host against its OWN NUMBER of guards, because the city figure
    conflates per-body power with the headcount the cap allows.
    THE BASELINE, measured before changing anything:
      m20   3 v 23 city -> wiped, killed nobody   ·  3v3 even -> 0.3:1
      m50   5 v 23 city -> 2.2:1                  ·  5v5 even -> FLAWLESS
      m100  7 v 23 city -> FLAWLESS               ·  7v7 even -> FLAWLESS
      lich 12 v 23 city -> FLAWLESS               · 12v12 even -> FLAWLESS
    Seven bound bodies erase a 23-strong garrison without a single loss, and the even
    fight shows per-body power is already runaway at m50, not just late. A Bone Knight
    goes atk 24 -> 141 across that range.
    UPKEEP is in, and split the way you asked: only `crafted` bodies (the binding
    circle) pay, at 0.25 Mortal Remains per point of bind weight per day. A body raised
    off a battlefield corpse pays NOTHING — `castRaise` never sets `crafted`, so the
    split was already there to key off. One gravehound costs 0.25/day; four Bone
    Knights cost 2.0/day; a late host of fifteen runs about 5.5/day, which is two or
    three bodies rendered. Run out and the cheapest body comes apart where it stands,
    one a day, never a lieutenant — a warning you can act on rather than a host that
    evaporates.
    HONEST NOTE: upkeep does NOT move the exchange rate, and was never going to — it is
    an economy lever, so it changes how long a host can be kept and how much corpse
    throughput it demands, not what happens when it meets a garrison. The harness still
    reports FLAWLESS at m100. Those numbers are printed as WATCH_ lines rather than
    assertions on purpose: failing the suite on a number the current change is not
    trying to move would make the suite lie. They become assertions when the `m` curve
    is bent, which is the change that actually moves them.

[DONE] Binding Circle balance — levers 1 + 2 from "The Binding Circle Ledger".
    The artifact's audit was verified line by line against the source before anything was
    built, and every load-bearing claim held: `d *= 1 + atk * 0.03` at line 2085, hitChance
    clamped to [0.15, 0.94], toughness saturating at exactly 75 (0.45/0.006), and castRaise
    already carrying the clamp AND the comment that states the design intent.
    ITS DIAGNOSIS WAS SHARPER THAN THE ONE IN THE EARLIER BRAINSTORM, which called the curve
    "linear and uncapped". It is QUADRATIC: craftUndead scales both clawDmg and atk with the
    caster's magic, and atk is then a 3%-per-point damage multiplier four thousand lines away,
    so damage is a product of two linear terms. That interaction is the whole bug and the
    earlier pass missed it by reading the stat line and stopping there.

    LEVER 2 — the double-dip. `atk` was doing two jobs under one name: to-hit (which clamps at
    0.94 and stops paying around enemy def + 20) and a damage multiplier that never stops.
    Bound bodies now carry a SEPARATE `dmgAtk` for the multiplier, saturating, while `atk`
    itself keeps rising so they can still land a blow on plate. Nothing else in the world sets
    `dmgAtk`, so nothing else changed.

    LEVER 1 — the reference body. Mortal Remains carry a quality now: a running measure of the
    fighting worth of what was rendered into them, weighted by yield and multiplied by rot the
    same way castRaise does it. The binding clamps every stat to a ceiling derived from it.
    Kept as ONE rolling number rather than making remains non-fungible — a stash of
    individually-tracked bone is a much larger inventory change than this problem is worth.
    It deliberately does NOT live in `stash`: five separate places walk Object.keys(stash), and
    a quality figure in there would have rendered as a phantom item you could try to sell.
    Capped at 46 (about an elite mercenary) so the plateau cannot be farmed off by rendering
    bosses — without that the rule is unbounded, just slower.

    MEASURED, on a realistic bandit diet (before -> after):
      m20   0.3:1 even  ->  0.3:1     knight atk 24
      m50   FLAWLESS    ->  0.8:1     knight atk 35
      m100  FLAWLESS    ->  2.3:1     knight atk 35   (was 96)
      lich  FLAWLESS    ->  4.0:1     knight atk 35   (was 141)
    And the diet is now the ceiling, which is the point:
      fed rats     quality  9  ->  0.3:1   knight atk 20
      fed bandits  quality 22  ->  2.3:1   knight atk 35
      fed guards   quality 40  ->  FLAWLESS at 7v7   knight atk 56
      fed bosses   quality 46 (capped)     knight atk 63
    The early game is untouched, which was the constraint both the artifact and tools/host.js
    independently flagged: at magic 4-20 these bindings were already weak, so a flat nerf
    would have broken the early game to fix the late one.

    AND IT IS VISIBLE. The binding circle panel now states what the pile is worth, what that
    permits, and that rendering better dead raises it — a ceiling the player cannot see is not
    a mechanic, it is an unexplained nerf.

    NOT DONE, from the same document: Part II (bone bows for the Marrow Archer — Sinew-Drawn,
    Rib-Lattice, Quiver-Born, Splinter-Host, Cairn-Warden). Worth doing, and the artifact's
